**Why an `apps/` folder split by domain (not by layer):** Each of your functional requirements (FR1–FR9) maps to a distinct business capability with its own models, serializers, and views. Splitting Django apps this way — one app per bounded concern (users, vendors, products, reservations, orders, payments, reviews, notifications, reports) — keeps each module independently testable and makes it obvious where new logic belongs as the system grows. It also mirrors your ER diagram (User, Vendor, Product, Reservation, Rental, Order, Payment, Review) almost one-to-one.

**Why `config/` is separate from `apps/`:** Settings, URL routing, and WSGI/ASGI entry points are project-wide concerns, not business logic. Isolating them avoids circular-import headaches and makes environment-specific settings (dev/prod) easy to swap — important since your NFRs call out separate performance/security postures per environment.

**Why `services.py` files inside some apps:** Requirements like "prevent double-booking" (FR4) or "apply student discount" (FR5) are business rules that don't belong in views or serializers directly. Pulling this logic into a `services.py` keeps views thin and makes the rules unit-testable in isolation — useful given your data-integrity requirements in section 6.4.

**Why `common/`:** Role-based permissions (Student/Vendor/Admin) and pagination are used by nearly every app. Centralizing them avoids duplicating access-control logic, which matters a lot for a system with three distinct user roles and strict RBAC requirements (section 9, Security).

**Why `requirements/` split by environment:** Keeps production lean (no dev/test tooling) while still letting local development install linters, pytest, etc.

