**Why `pages/` is split by role (student/vendor/admin):** Section 8 of your SRS lists distinct dashboards and pages per user class. Since permissions and navigation differ sharply by role, grouping pages this way keeps route-protection logic simple and matches how a developer would mentally navigate "what does a vendor see" vs "what does a student see."

**Why `api/` is separated from `components/`:** Keeps network/data-fetching logic out of UI components entirely, so components stay reusable and testable with mock data, and API changes (e.g., swapping Axios config or endpoint paths) don't ripple through the UI layer.

**Why `context/` for auth:** JWT-based auth with three roles means the whole app needs to know "who is logged in and what can they do." A single AuthContext avoids prop-drilling this through every page.

**Why `routes/ProtectedRoute.jsx`:** Your RBAC requirement (Section 9) needs enforcement on both backend and frontend — the frontend guard just prevents unauthorized users from *seeing* pages they shouldn't, complementing the backend's actual enforcement.

